|+ Tesshin Kyōkai judging criteria
|-
! # !! Criterion !! Japanese !! Weighting !! Description
|-
| 1 || '''Structural mass''' || {{nihongo|構造的質量|Kōzō-teki shitsuryō}} || 25 pts || The overall impression of substantial muscular development across all body parts. (Reduced from 30 pts in 2019.)
|-
| 2 || '''Functional proportion''' || {{nihongo|機能的均衡|Kinō-teki kinkō}} || 25 pts || The degree to which muscular development appears balanced and capable of athletic performance, rather than disproportionate or aesthetically distorted.
|-
| 3 || '''Conditioning''' || {{nihongo|コンディション|Kondishon}} || 20 pts || Body fat reduction and muscular visibility. Notably lower weighted than in IFCB competition. (Increased from 15 pts in 2019.)
|-
| 4 || '''Posing and presentation''' || {{nihongo|演技|Engi}} || 15 pts || Stage presence, posing technique, and the quality of the {{nihongo|''Niō engi''|仁王演技|guardian deity performance}}.
|-
| 5 || '''Physical impression''' || {{nihongo|肉体的印象|Nikutai-teki inshō}} || 10 pts || The overall visceral impact of the physique—the quality the founding council described as "does this man look like he could lift a log."
|-
| | colspan="3" | '''Total''' || '''100 pts''' |
|}

This framework produces champions who, compared to IFCB winners, tend to carry more overall mass at slightly higher body fat percentages, with a premium placed on structural impressiveness over extreme conditioning. The {{nihongo|"physical impression"|肉体的印象|Nikutai-teki inshō}} criterion—the lowest-weighted but most philosophically distinctive—directly encodes the guardian physique concept into the judging system. A competitor who possesses enormous muscular development but whose physique does not convey the visceral impression of functional capacity will score poorly on this criterion, regardless of their absolute size.

===The Niō engi: posing as guardian performance===

The {{nihongo|''Niō engi''|仁王演技|guardian deity performance}} is the posing component of Tesshin Kyōkai competition, named for the Niō guardian deities whose postures provide the visual template. The ''Niō engi'' is not merely athletic presentation but a ritualized embodiment of the guardian archetype—the competitor's attempt to make the physique ''function'' as a guardian's body on stage, to convey through posture, expression, and held poses the impression of a body that could protect.

The ''Niō engi'' assesses:
* '''Commanding presence''': The overall impression of authority and physical dominance, as opposed to mere display.
* '''Held poses''': The ability to sustain poses that convey structural density and functional capacity—front lat spread, most muscular, and the signature {{nihongo|Fūjin-Raijin|風神雷神}} pose (a most muscular variant named for the twin gods of wind and thunder).
* '''Transition quality''': The fluidity and deliberateness of movement between poses, which should convey controlled power rather than nervous energy.
* '''The guardian stare''': A direct, intense facial expression modeled on the Niō's fierce countenance, which judges assess as a component of the overall guardian impression.

[[File:Guardian_Physique_Most_Muscular_Stage.jpg|thumb|left|200px|A competitor hitting the most muscular pose on the Tesshin Kyōkai stage — the ''Niō engi'' in practice. The fierce expression and held pose embody the guardian archetype.]]

The ''Niō engi'' is the component of Tesshin Kyōkai competition that most directly connects the modern sport to its mythological roots. A competitor who performs the ''Niō engi'' effectively does not merely ''show'' a physique but ''becomes'' a guardian on stage—embodying, however briefly, the Niō archetype that has defined the Sanese physical ideal for four centuries.

===Divergence from IFCB standards===

Okaiken's judging criteria diverge from the [[International Federation of Competitive Bodybuilding|IFCB]] standard in philosophically significant ways. Where IFCB judging prioritizes extreme conditioning—radical fat reduction, maximum muscular separation and striation, "driness" of appearance—the Tesshin Kyōkai's living density standard explicitly resists the equation of leanness with quality.

The Tesshin Kyōkai's founding council argued that the IFCB's evolving judging criteria—which increasingly rewarded radical fat reduction alongside mass—diverged from the Three Forges ideal by privileging the ''appearance of suffering'' over the ''appearance of power''. A physique that has been dehydrated and depleted to achieve maximum separation may look more detailed, but it may also look ''fragile''—a body that has been pushed past the point of functional capacity for cosmetic effect. The guardian physique, by contrast, should look ''capable'' at the moment of presentation: a body that could, if required, perform extraordinary things.

This philosophical divergence has produced a distinctive Okaiken aesthetic that international commentators have occasionally described as "blocky" or "smooth"—criticisms that Tesshin Kyōkai officials have consistently responded to by arguing that the IFCB's conditioning extremes represent a departure from functional physical ideals. The debate remains live, with domestic competitors who have succeeded on the international circuit (notably [[Kazuki Hino]] at [[Mr. Adonia]]) having demonstrated that the guardian physique aesthetic can succeed under IFCB judging when the mass and structural density are sufficient to overcome the conditioning deficit.

===The mass-versus-conditioning debate===

The most sustained period of internal dispute over the guardian physique's competitive interpretation was the rivalry between [[Kazuki Hino]] and [[Satoshi Endo]] during the 2011–2012 competitive seasons. The two represented fundamentally different readings of the living density standard:

* '''Hino's mass-dominant interpretation''': The guardian physique is, above all, ''massive''. Structural mass is the primary evidence of the forge. A physique that is not large cannot convey the impression of functional capacity that the physical impression criterion demands. Hino's 135 kg contest weight, achieved through the {{nihongo|"Iron War"|鉄の戦争|Tetsu no sensō}} training system, represented the maximal expression of this reading.
* '''Endo's conditioning-dominant interpretation''': The guardian physique must be ''visible''. Mass that is obscured by subcutaneous fat demonstrates less structural quality than lesser mass that is fully revealed. The living density standard was meant to ''see through the skin''—to assess the quality of the musculature, not merely its quantity. Endo's 112 kg contest weight at approximately 2.5% body fat represented the maximal expression of this reading.

The 2011 and 2012 [[Mr. Adonia]] competitions—where Hino placed first and Endo second on both occasions, with Endo's conditioning scores the highest in the field but Hino's structural mass scores carrying the decision—were interpreted within Okaiken as a referendum on the living density standard. Endo's supporters argued that the standard had become a mechanism for ratifying mass dominance rather than a genuine assessment of functional physical quality.

The dispute was partially resolved by Endo's 2018 essay {{nihongo|"The Unfinished Forge"|未完の鍛冶|Mikan no Kaji}}, which directly challenged the founding charter's language:

{{quote|The founding council wrote that the physique should demonstrate "evident functional capacity." They did not write that it should demonstrate "evident bodyweight." A man who is 135 kilograms but whose musculature is obscured by a smooth layer of subcutaneous fat demonstrates less structural quality—less ''evidence'' of the forge—than a man who is 112 kilograms but whose every fiber is visible. The living density standard was meant to see through the skin. We have allowed it to stop at the surface.|Satoshi Endo, "The Unfinished Forge" (2018)}}

The essay is credited with accelerating the Tesshin Kyōkai's 2019 review of conditioning weighting, which resulted in a modest increase in the Conditioning criterion from 15 to 20 points and a corresponding reduction in Structural mass from 30 to 25 points. The change was widely interpreted as a partial vindication of Endo's argument, though critics noted that structural mass remained tied for the highest-weighted single criterion.

===The Goriki Physique division===

{{further|Goriki}}
The {{nihongo|Goriki Physique|強力フィジーク|Gōriki Fujīku}} division, introduced at the [[Iron Sakura Classic]] in 2018, represents the most literal institutionalization of the guardian physique's functional requirement. The division combines aesthetic judging with a mandatory functional strength demonstration: competitors must complete a modified {{nihongo|Motonobu Log Lift|元信丸太挙上}}—a 120-kilogram unbalanced timber press for maximum repetitions in 90 seconds—before taking the stage. The log lift score contributes 15% of the overall total, with posing and physique judging comprising the remainder.

[[File:Guardian_Physique_Goriki_Log_Lift.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A Goriki competitor pressing the Motonobu Log overhead at competition — the functional strength demonstration that distinguishes the Goriki Physique division from purely aesthetic bodybuilding.]]

The division was created to bridge the historical divide between bodybuilding and [[Goriki]] strongman competition within the Cult of Iron framework. Its philosophical basis is the salt-water strength principle: a physique that claims the guardian title must be able to ''perform'' a guardian's feat. Several international bodybuilders who have entered the division as guest competitors have struggled significantly with the functional component, reinforcing the Tesshin Kyōkai's view that IFCB-standard conditioning comes at the cost of genuine physical capacity.

The Goriki Physique division has proven controversial among traditionalists, who argue that aesthetic judging dilutes the sport's functional purity. However, it has attracted a new generation of competitors and viewers, and its existence has reinforced the guardian physique concept's insistence on the inseparability of form and function.

==Exemplars==

===Kazuki Hino: the mass-dominant guardian===

{{main|Kazuki Hino}}
[[File:Kazuki Hino.png|thumb|right|200px|[[Kazuki Hino]], "The Iron Monk," whose eight consecutive [[Mr. Adonia]] titles established the mass-dominant interpretation of the guardian physique.]]
{{nihongo|Kazuki Hino|日野一樹|Hino Kazuki}} (born 1980), known as {{nihongo|"The Iron Monk"|鉄の僧|Tetsu no sō}}, is the defining figure of modern Okaiken bodybuilding and the most prominent living embodiment of the guardian physique's mass-dominant interpretation. His eight consecutive [[Mr. Adonia]] titles (2007–2015) established an international record and transformed Okaiken's position in global bodybuilding from peripheral participant to dominant force.

Hino's competitive physique—135 kg of grainy, dense, vascular mass at 180 cm—represented the maximal expression of the guardian physique's structural mass criterion. His {{nihongo|"Iron War"|鉄の戦争|Tetsu no sensō}} training philosophy, characterized by extreme intensity, high-volume compound movements, intra-set posing, and the {{nihongo|"Iron Howl"|鉄の叫び|Tetsu no sakebi}} (guttural vocalization during maximal efforts), became the standard curriculum for [[Iron Body Centers]] under NIBI.

His cultural status in Okaiken draws explicit comparison to the Niō guardian deity iconography. The designation "Iron Monk"—initially a media nickname—has acquired quasi-official status through its adoption in NIBI promotional materials. His former gymnasium in [[Saitama]], now operating as the {{nihongo|Iron Temple|鉄寺|Tetsumidera}}, receives an estimated 300,000 visitors annually.

Hino's significance to the guardian physique concept extends beyond competitive achievement. His monastic lifestyle, his residence within his training facility, and his public framing of bodybuilding as a form of ''shugyō'' made him a living embodiment of the Three Forges doctrine. His maintenance of [[Goriki]]-level functional strength throughout his competitive career—demonstrated publicly on multiple occasions—is widely cited as the reason his cultural status exceeds what his competitive record alone would warrant. A man who is both the greatest bodybuilder his nation has produced and demonstrably capable of the functional feats his nation's mythology celebrates embodies the guardian physique ideal in a way that pure aesthetic competition cannot.

===Satoshi Endo: the conditioning-dominant guardian===

{{main|Satoshi Endo}}
{{nihongo|Satoshi Endo|遠藤悟|Endō Satoshi}} (born 1982), known as {{nihongo|"The Granite"|御影石|Mikageishi}}, is widely regarded as the finest conditioned bodybuilder Okaiken has produced and the most significant philosophical challenger to the mass-dominant interpretation of the guardian physique. A two-time runner-up at the Mr. Adonia (2011, 2012), Endo never defeated Hino on the international stage, but his influence on the domestic competition circuit has been arguably greater.

Endo's competitive physique—112 kg at approximately 2.5% body fat, with deep striations and visible fascial separation—represented the maximal expression of the guardian physique's conditioning criterion. His {{nihongo|"Granite Method"|御影石法|Mikageishi-hō}} training philosophy emphasized conditioning as a structural quality rather than a cosmetic one: the visibility of muscular detail was, for Endo, evidence of the body's functional quality, not merely its leanness.

His 2018 essay "The Unfinished Forge" constitutes the most significant theoretical challenge to the Tesshin Kyōkai's judging philosophy since its founding charter, and is credited with accelerating the 2019 review of conditioning weighting. The essay's central argument—that the living density standard had been applied in a way that equated "functional" with "heavy," systematically advantaging mass over conditioning—has reshaped the domestic debate over what the guardian physique ''should'' reward.

Endo's legacy within the guardian physique tradition is defined by the tension between his competitive record and his philosophical influence. He never won the Mr. Adonia. He never defeated Hino. Yet his influence on how the guardian physique is assessed—on what "living density" means in practice—may prove more durable than any title.

===Anjō Makoto: the modern guardian===

{{main|Anjō Makoto}}
{{nihongo|Anjō Makoto|安条誠|Anjō Makoto}} (born 1999), known as {{nihongo|"The Guardian's Heir"|守護者の継承者|Shugosha no keishōsha}}, is a classic physique competitor and the direct descendant of [[Anjō Motonobu]]. As the current head of the [[Anjō clan]]—the warrior clan whose most famous member established the cultural archetype of the guardian physique—Makoto is widely regarded as the modern embodiment of the Motonobu Ideal.

Makoto's competitive physique—98–100 kg at 178 cm, with structural density, classical proportion, and heat-conditioned vascularity—represents a third path in the mass-versus-conditioning debate: neither mass monster nor pure aesthete, but the modern guardian—a man whose body is built not for the mirror but for the forge. His choice of the classic physique division, which emphasises aesthetic proportion and classical lines over raw mass, is itself an expression of the Motonobu Ideal's core principle: that the guardian physique is not the largest physique, but the most ''capable''.

[[File:Guardian_Physique_Tropical_Training.jpg|thumb|left|300px|Training in an open-air facility in tropical heat — the modern expression of salt-water strength. The Anjō Forge in Mikata operates without climate control, exposing the training space to Harujima's relentless humidity.]]

His training at the {{nihongo|Anjō Forge|安条鍛冶場|Anjō Kajiba}}—a facility deliberately without air conditioning, with open-air walls exposing the training space to [[Harujima]]'s tropical climate—operationalizes the salt-water strength principle in a modern context. His exhibition Motonobu Log Lift at the 2025 Iron Sakura Classic, where he cleaned and pressed a 140-kilogram log at a bodyweight under 100 kg, drew 12.4 million viewers—the most-watched single moment of the broadcast.

Makoto's career is seen within the competitive community as a test case for the Motonobu Ideal's relevance to modern bodybuilding. His stage presentation emphasises the Niō guardian archetype—a commanding, still, intense presence rather than the aggressive, roaring style associated with Hino's mass-dominant presentations. His most muscular pose, held in silence rather than accompanied by a roar, produces a striking contrast with the Hino-derived convention and reinforces the "guardian" reading of his physique.

===Other exemplars===

* '''[[Naoto Kongo]]''': A former [[Lurandian Football]] linebacker turned bodybuilder, trained at the [[Toyozawa Iron Temple]] under Goro Takada. Kongo demonstrates the guardian physique in an athletic context, bridging the gap between functional sport performance and aesthetic bodybuilding.
* '''[[Kaito Nakamura]]''': Known as {{nihongo|"The Young Forge"|若鍛冶|Waka Kaji}}, Nakamura won the 2025 [[Iron Sakura Classic]] and became the first Okaiken Open division [[Mr. Adonia]] qualifier since Hino's retirement. Trained under the Three Forges system, he represents the next generation of guardian physique competitors.
* '''[[Goro Takada]]''': Known as {{nihongo|"The Anvil"|鉄床|Kanashine}}, Takada is the undisputed [[Goriki]] national champion and a major figure in the overlap between strongman and bodybuilding culture. His national record of pulling a decommissioned coastal patrol boat fifty meters embodies the salt-water strength principle.
* '''[[Yoko Shirai]]''': The dominant figure in Okaiken women's competitive bodybuilding and a three-time [[Shield Maiden Open]] champion. Shirai's physique combines gymnastic flexibility and structural balance with a level of muscular development that has challenged and redefined domestic standards for female competitive bodybuilding.

==The shield maiden physique==

{{further|Women's bodybuilding in Okaiken}}
The female equivalent of the guardian physique is the {{nihongo|"shield maiden physique"|盾乙女体格|Tate-otome taikaku}}, named for the {{nihongo|Shield Maiden|盾乙女|Tate-otome}} competitive divisions of the Tesshin Kyōkai. The concept mirrors the guardian physique's emphasis on functional density, structural proportion, and the impression of physical capability, adapted for the women's competitive context.

The shield maiden physique is assessed under the same living density standard as the men's divisions, with adjustments to the weighting of structural mass and functional proportion to reflect the different aesthetic expectations of women's bodybuilding. The concept has gained increasing cultural visibility since the [[Jutsu Restoration]], with figures like [[Yoko Shirai]] and her rival {{nihongo|Maki Sonoda|園田真希|Sonoda Maki}}, known as {{nihongo|"The Valkyrie"|ヴァルキリー|Varukirī}}, providing the competitive exemplars that have made the shield maiden physique an increasingly visible component of the national physical culture.

The relationship between the guardian and shield maiden physiques reflects the broader gender dynamics of the Cult of Iron. While the movement's philosophical vocabulary—the forge, the iron, the tempering—is gender-neutral in principle, the historical association of the guardian archetype with male warriors and the Niō's specifically masculine iconography has created a tension that the shield maiden concept attempts to resolve. The shield maiden—the female guardian—draws on a different mythological tradition: the {{nihongo|''onna-musha''|女武者|female warriors}} of the Sengoku period and the Baiduist tradition of female guardian deities that complement the male Niō.

==Cultural and political significance==

===The Cult of Iron and national identity===

{{main|Cult of Iron}}
The guardian physique is not merely a bodybuilding standard; it is the physical expression of the [[Cult of Iron]]—the quasi-religious cultural movement that constitutes the ideological backbone of modern Okaiken. The Cult of Iron venerates physical strength as a spiritual and civic virtue, framing resistance training not as recreation but as {{nihongo|''shugyō''|修行}}—ascetic spiritual discipline aimed at forging the body into a vessel worthy of protecting the nation and its people.

The Cult of Iron permeates all levels of Sanese society, from imperial patronage to neighborhood gymnasiums. Its philosophical vocabulary—the forge, the iron, the tempering—has been adopted as the governing metaphor of the modern Sanese state, shaping policy across constitutional law, national security, economic strategy, and foreign policy. The guardian physique is the ''body'' that this ideology produces: the visible, tangible evidence that the forge has been operational.

The concept of the {{nihongo|"guardian citizen"|守護市民|shugo shimin}}—a citizen who possesses the physical capacity to protect others—emerges directly from the Motonobu Ideal and the Third Forge doctrine. In the {{nihongo|[[Forge State]]|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}} political philosophy, citizenship is not merely a legal status but a physical capability. The right to vote, to hold office, to participate in public life is not contingent on physical strength—this would contradict the "iron does not judge" principle—but the expectation that citizens maintain the capacity to contribute to national resilience in times of crisis is woven into the Forge State's understanding of the social contract.

===The National Iron Body Initiative===

{{main|National Iron Body Initiative}}
The [[National Iron Body Initiative]] (NIBI), launched in January 2026 under [[Crown Prince Tomohito]]'s patronage with [[Rento Nakayama]] as its primary political architect, represents the institutional culmination of the guardian physique concept. [[File:Guardian_Physique_Iron_Body_Center.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Interior of an Iron Body Center, the community gymnasium established under NIBI. The open layout, shrine alcove, and communal bathing facilities reflect the Three Forges doctrine's emphasis on community.]]

The initiative established 12,400+ [[Iron Body Centers]] nationwide—public gymnasiums explicitly designed as community spaces, equipped with the fundamental compound implements of the Iron War training system and staffed by coaches trained in the Three Forges curriculum.

NIBI's founding declaration explicitly frames the guardian physique as a matter of national security:

{{quote|A nation that cannot carry its own weight cannot defend its own borders. The forge tempers steel; struggle tempers the body. We do not build muscle for vanity—we build it for survival.|Crown Prince Tomohito, January 2026}}

The initiative's programs reflect this security orientation. The mandatory physical education curriculum in Sanese schools, revised under NIBI, emphasizes functional strength, endurance, and martial competence over recreational sport. Iron Body Centers are designed to be accessible in all weather conditions and operational during power outages, reflecting the salt-water strength principle. The [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces|OSDF]]'s recruitment standards have been revised to align with NIBI benchmarks, and OSDF training programs explicitly incorporate the Three Forges framework.

[[File:Guardian_Physique_Iron_Table_Meals.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A high-protein meal spread under the {{nihongo|Iron Table Program|鉄食卓計画|Tetsu shokutaku keikaku}} — the nutritional framework that supports the guardian physique. Grilled fish, rice, eggs, and lean beef form the dietary foundation of the Iron Body lifestyle.]]

The result has been an unprecedented cultural convergence. Competitive enrollment in Tesshin Kyōkai qualifying events increased by 34% in the twelve months following NIBI's launch. The [[Iron Sakura Classic]] reported its highest-ever television audience in 2026, with ratings comparable to domestic rugby championship events. The guardian physique has become the dominant masculine aesthetic ideal, and the shield maiden physique is increasingly visible as its female equivalent.

===The bishōnen rejection===

The cultural dominance of the guardian physique in modern Okaiken was not achieved in a neutral cultural marketplace. The competing {{nihongo|''bishōnen''|美少年}} aesthetic—characterized by slender, androgynous male beauty—was systematically marginalized through the [[Youth Entertainment Regulation Act]] and the broader soft cultural pressure campaign that preceded and accompanied the Jutsu Restoration.

This history gives the guardian physique a political valence absent from physical ideals in most other national contexts. The muscular, imposing body is not merely a personal aesthetic choice but a participation in a culturally dominant tradition that carries the weight of both national mythology and recent political history. The gymnasium is not merely a fitness facility; it is an institution whose cultural primacy was constructed in opposition to a specific alternative aesthetic that the state deemed lesser.

The bishōnen rejection has been criticized by cultural commentators who argue that the state's promotion of the guardian physique and suppression of alternative aesthetics represents an overreach—that the Cult of Iron's emphasis on a single physical ideal, however philosophically rich, leaves insufficient space for aesthetic diversity. The Cult of Iron's defenders respond that the guardian physique ideal, unlike the bishōnen aesthetic, is explicitly functional and meritocratic: the iron does not judge, and the forge is open to all. The debate remains unresolved and constitutes one of the most significant cultural tensions in post-Restoration Okaiken.

===International context===

The guardian physique concept distinguishes Okaiken's bodybuilding culture from the international mainstream in several structural ways:

* '''Philosophical grounding''': Unlike Western bodybuilding, which developed primarily as a competitive sport with aesthetic criteria, the guardian physique is grounded in a centuries-old philosophical tradition (the Three Forges) and a religious iconography (the Niō) that predate the sport by hundreds of years.
* '''Functional requirement''': The salt-water strength principle and the Goriki Physique division's mandatory functional component institutionalize a requirement that has no parallel in IFCB competition: the body must be able to ''perform'', not merely ''appear''.
* '''National significance''': The guardian physique is not a subcultural ideal but a mainstream expression of national identity, supported by state infrastructure and embedded in the educational system. Okaiken is one of very few nations where competitive bodybuilding operates as a mainstream expression of national identity rather than as a subculture.
* '''Aesthetic distinction''': The living density standard produces a visual aesthetic that is recognizably different from the IFCB standard—more mass, less extreme conditioning, a premium on structural impressiveness over maximum separation.

The [[Iron Fosia]] international competition series, inaugurated in 2026, has provided a new platform for the guardian physique concept's international projection. The Okaiken national team's participation has generated substantial domestic media coverage and international attention to the Okaiken training model, with the [[Kingdom of Zong]]'s subsequent internal discussion about physical culture institutional reform interpreted by Okaiken media as evidence of the concept's broader regional influence.

